nto the original equation: cos2x + cos6x + 2sin\u00b2x\u2248\u221a3/2 +0 +0.13398\u22480.866 +0.13398\u22481.0, which is correct. So yes, x=11\u03c0/12 is a solution.\n\nBut is there any other solution in the interval?\n\nWait, perhaps I missed something. Let me check the second case again.\n\nThe equation cos4x=1/2. So 4x= \u00b1\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0n. So x= \u00b1\u03c0/12 + \u03c0n/2.\n\nWe found that x=11\u03c0/12 is one solution. Let's check if 4x=\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0n or 4x=-\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0n.\n\nFor x in [5\u03c0/6, \u03c0], let's compute 4x: since x is between ~2.618 and 3.1416, so 4x is between ~10.472 and ~12.5664. Which is between approximately 10.472 and 12.5664 radians.\n\nWe need cos4x=1/2 in this interval. So angles where cosine is 1/2 are at \u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k and -\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k.\n\nSo let's find all angles \u03b8=4x in [10.472,12.5664] such that \u03b8=\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k or \u03b8=-\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k.\n\nLet me compute \u03b8=\u03c0/3\u22481.0472, but we need \u03b8 in [10.472,12.5664]. Let's find k such that \u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k is in that interval.\n\nCompute (10.472 - \u03c0/3)/2\u03c0\u2248(10.472 -1.0472)/6.283\u22489.4248/6.283\u22481.5. So k=2:\n\n\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0*2= \u03c0/3 +4\u03c0\u22481.0472 +12.566\u224813.6132, which is above 12.5664.\n\nk=1: \u03c0/3 +2\u03c0*1\u22481.0472+6.283\u22487.330, which is below 10.472.\n\nk=2: too high. Similarly for negative side:\n\n\u03b8=-\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k. Let's see. -\u03c0/3\u2248-1.0472.\n\nFind k such that -\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0k is in [10.472,12.5664].\n\nCompute (10.472 +\u03c0/3)/2\u03c0\u2248(10.472 +1.0472)/6.283\u224811.5192/6.283\u22481.833. So k=2:\n\n-\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0*2= -1.0472 +12.566\u224811.5188, which is in [10.472,12.5664].\n\nSo \u03b8=11.5188 radians. Then x=\u03b8/4\u224811.5188/4\u22482.8797, which is 11\u03c0/12\u22482.8798. That's the solution we found earlier.\n\nNext k=3: -\u03c0/3 +2\u03c0*3= -1.0472 +18.849\u224817.8018, which is higher than 12.5664. So only k=2 gives a solutio