is at 5\u03c0/6. So if we parametrize \u03b8 from 3\u03c0/2 to 5\u03c0/6 clockwise, that covers arc DF. \n\nBut in our parametrization, point P is (1 + (\u221a3/3) cos \u03b8, \u221a3/3 + (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8). Let me check when \u03b8 = 3\u03c0/2 (270 degrees):\n\ncos \u03b8 = 0, sin \u03b8 = -1\n\nSo x = 1 + 0 = 1, y = \u221a3/3 - \u221a3/3 = 0. Which is point D(1,0). Correct.\n\nWhen \u03b8 = 5\u03c0/6 (150 degrees):\n\ncos \u03b8 = cos 5\u03c0/6 = -\u221a3/2, sin \u03b8 = sin 5\u03c0/6 = 1/2\n\nx = 1 + (\u221a3/3)(-\u221a3/2) = 1 - (3/6) = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5\n\ny = \u221a3/3 + (\u221a3/3)(1/2) = \u221a3/3 + \u221a3/6 = (2\u221a3 + \u221a3)/6 = (3\u221a3)/6 = \u221a3/2\n\nWhich is point F(0.5, \u221a3/2). Correct.\n\nSo parametrization is correct. Now, compute d\u2081:\n\nd\u2081 = |2\u221a3/3 + cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8| / 2\n\nBut since P is on arc DF, which is from D(3\u03c0/2) to F(5\u03c0/6), \u03b8 ranges from 3\u03c0/2 to 5\u03c0/6. Let's see the expression inside the absolute value:\n\n2\u221a3/3 + cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8\n\nAt \u03b8 = 3\u03c0/2:\n\n2\u221a3/3 + 0 - (\u221a3/3)(-1) = 2\u221a3/3 + \u221a3/3 = \u221a3\n\nAt \u03b8 = 5\u03c0/6:\n\n2\u221a3/3 + cos(5\u03c0/6) - (\u221a3/3) sin(5\u03c0/6) = 2\u221a3/3 + (-\u221a3/2) - (\u221a3/3)(1/2) = 2\u221a3/3 - \u221a3/2 - \u221a3/6\n\nConvert to common denominator of 6:\n\n= (4\u221a3/6 - 3\u221a3/6 - \u221a3/6) = 0\n\nSo between \u03b8 = 3\u03c0/2 and 5\u03c0/6, the expression inside the absolute value starts at \u221a3 and decreases to 0. Since it's always positive in between (since P is below AC except at F where it's zero), the absolute value can be removed, and d\u2081 = (2\u221a3/3 + cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8)/2.\n\nTherefore, d\u2081 = [2\u221a3/3 + cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2\n\nSimilarly, d\u2082 = \u221a3/3 + (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8\n\nd\u2083: distance to BC. Let's compute that:\n\nd\u2083 = | -\u221a3 x - y + 2\u221a3 | / 2\n\nSubstituting x and y:\n\n=