, d\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nd\u2083 = [2\u221a3/3 - cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2\n\nNotice that d\u2081 and d\u2083 have similar structures. Let me see:\n\nLet me denote A = 2\u221a3/3, B = cos \u03b8, C = (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8.\n\nThen d\u2081 = (A - C + B)/2, d\u2083 = (A - B - C)/2\n\nAnd d\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nBut maybe this notation isn't helpful. Let me instead look for a relationship between d\u2081, d\u2082, and d\u2083. \n\nObserve that:\n\nd\u2081 + d\u2083 = [2\u221a3/3 + cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8 + 2\u221a3/3 - cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2\n\n= [4\u221a3/3 - 2*(\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2\n\n= [4\u221a3/3 - (2\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2\n\n= [2\u221a3/3 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]\n\nBut d\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nSo d\u2081 + d\u2083 = 2\u221a3/3 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8 = \u221a3/3 (2 - sin \u03b8 )\n\nBut d\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nNot sure if this helps directly. Let's think about squaring (\u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082)^2 and see if it equals d\u2083.\n\n(\u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082)^2 = d\u2081 + d\u2082 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082)\n\nWe need to show this equals d\u2083.\n\nSo if we can show that d\u2081 + d\u2082 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = d\u2083, then it's proved.\n\nGiven that d\u2081 + d\u2083 = \u221a3/3 (2 - sin \u03b8 )\n\nFrom earlier, d\u2081 + d\u2083 = 2\u221a3/3 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8\n\nAnd d\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nSo d\u2081 + d\u2082 + d\u2083 = (2\u221a3/3 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8 ) + (\u221a3/3)(1 + sin \u03b8 ) = 2\u221a3/3 + \u221a3/3 - (\u221a3/3 sin \u03b8 ) + \u221a3/3 sin \u03b8 = 3\u221a3/3 = \u221a3. Which matches the known result that the sum of distances from any interior point to the three sides is 3r = \u221a3 (since r = \u221a3/3). But we are dealing with d\u2081 + d\u2082 + d\u2083 = \u221a3. However, the relation we need is (\u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082)^2 = d\u2083. \n\nSo let's compute d\u2081 + d\u2082 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082):\n\n= (d\u2081 + d\u2082) + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082)\n\nBut d\u2081 + d\u2082 = \u221a3 - d\u2083 (since d\u2081 + d\u2082 + d\u2083 = \u221a3)\n\nTherefore, (\u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082)^2 = (\u221a3 - d\u2083) + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082)\n\nWe need this to equal d\u2083:\n\n\u221a3 - d\u2083 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = d\u2083\n\n\u2192 \u221a3 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = 2d\u2083\n\n\u2192 (\u221a3)/2 + \u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = d\u2083\n\nBut this seems like a dead end. Let me try another approach. Let's express d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083 in terms of sin \u03b8 and cos \u03b8 and see if the equation \u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082 = \u221ad\u2083 holds.\n\nFirst, let's express d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083:\n\nd\u2081 = [2\u221a3/3 + cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by 3 to eliminate denominators:\n\nd\u2081 = [2\u221a3 + 3 cos \u03b8 - \u221a3 sin \u03b8]/6\n\nSimilarly, d\u2082 = [\u221a3/3 + (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8 ] = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 ) = [\u221a3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )]/3\n\nd\u2083 = [2\u221a3/3 - cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/3) sin \u03b8]/2 = [2\u221a3 - 3 cos \u03b8 - \u221a3 sin \u03b8 ]/6\n\nNow, let's compute \u221ad\u2081, \u221ad\u2082, \u221ad\u2083:\n\nFirst, d\u2082 is [\u221a3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )]/3. So \u221ad\u2082 = \u221a[ \u221a3 (1 + sin \u03b8 ) / 3 ] = [ (3^{1/4}) \u221a(1 + sin \u03b8 ) ] / \u221a3.\n\nWait, maybe better to write:\n\nd\u2082 = (\u221a3 / 3)(1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nSo \u221ad\u2082 = \u221a(\u221a3 / 3) * \u221a(1 + sin \u03b8 ) = (3^{1/4}/\u221a3) * \u221a(1 + sin \u03b8 )\n\nBut this seems messy. Let's instead consider squaring both sides of the desired equation:\n\n\u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082 = \u221ad\u2083 \u2192 (\u221ad\u2081 + \u221ad\u2082)^2 = d\u2083 \u2192 d\u2081 + d\u2082 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = d\u2083\n\nBut from earlier, we know d\u2081 + d\u2082 + d\u2083 = \u221a3. So substituting d\u2083 = \u221a3 - d\u2081 - d\u2082 into the equation:\n\nd\u2081 + d\u2082 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = \u221a3 - d\u2081 - d\u2082\n\n\u2192 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = \u221a3 - 2(d\u2081 + d\u2082)\n\nBut I'm not sure if this helps. Let me instead try plugging in the expressions for d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083 into this equation.\n\nWe need to verify:\n\nd\u2081 + d\u2082 + 2\u221a(d\u2081 d\u2082) = d\u2083\n\nBut substituting the expressions:\n\nLeft side: [ (2\u221a3 + 3 cos \u03b8 - \u221a3 sin \u03b8)/6 ] + [ (\u221a3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )) / 3 ] + 2\u221a[ ( (2\u221a3 + 3 cos \u03b8 - \u221a3 sin \u03b8)/6 ) * ( (\u221a3 (1 + sin \u03b8 )) / 3 ) ]\n\nRight side: [2\u221a3 - 3 cos \u03b8 - \u221a3 sin \u03b8]/6\n\nThis looks very complicated. Perhaps there's a trigonometric identity or substitution that can simplify this. \n\nAlternatively, notice that the equation to prove is similar to an identity involving sine and cosine. Let me see if I can express d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083 in terms of sine and cosine of some angle related to \u03b8. \n\nAlternatively, let's make substitution \u03c6 = \u03b8 + \u03c0/2. Since the parametrization is using \u03b8, maybe a phase shift can simplify the expressions. Let me try:\n\nLet \u03c6 = \u03b8 + \u03c0/2. Then \u03b8 = \u03c6 - \u03c0/2. Let's substitute into expressions for d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083.\n\nFirst, compute sin \u03b8 and cos \u03b8:\n\nsin \u03b8 = sin(\u03c6 - \u03c0/2) = -cos \u03c6\n\ncos \u03b8 = cos(\u03c6 - \u03c0/2) = sin \u03c6\n\nSo:\n\nd\u2081 = [2\u221a3/3 + sin \u03c6 - (\u221a3/3)(-cos \u03c6)]/2 = [2\u221a3/3 + sin \u03c6 + (\u221a3/3 cos \u03c6)]/2\n\nd\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 + sin \u03b8 ) = \u221a3/3 (1 - cos \u03c6 )\n\nd\u2083 = [2\u221a3/3 - sin \u03c6 - (\u221a3/3)(-cos \u03c6)]/2 = [2\u221a3/3 - sin \u03c6 + (\u221a3/3 cos \u03c6)]/2\n\nSo substituting \u03c6, we have:\n\nd\u2081 = [2\u221a3/3 + sin \u03c6 + (\u221a3/3 cos \u03c6)]/2\n\nd\u2082 = \u221a3/3 (1 - cos \u03c6 )\n\nd\u2083 = [2\u221a3/3 - sin \u03c6 + (\u221a3/3 cos \u03c6)]/2\n\nNow, the e