s of the quadrilateral.\n   - **Scenario II**: Two sides are tangent to the circle at points outside the sides of the quadrilateral.\n   - **Scenario III**: All four sides are tangent to the circle at points outside the sides of the quadrilateral.\n\n4. **General Result**:\n   - Regardless of the position of the points of tangency (on the sides or their extensions), the sum of the angles \\(AOB\\) and \\(COD\\) is always \\(180^\\circ\\), and the sum of the angles \\(BOC\\) and \\(DOA\\) is also \\(180^\\circ\\). This is due to the fact that the sum of the internal angles of the quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\), and the angles at the center \\(O\\) are supplementary to half the sum of these internal angles.\n\n### Final Answer\n\nFor all scenarios I, II, and III, the sums are:\n\nI. \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\)\n\nII. \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\)\n\nIII. \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's analyze the two angles' sums in each event as described in the problem narrative.\n\n#### I. All Four Tangent Points are on the Sides of the Quadrilateral:\n\nGiven that a circle touches all four sides of the quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\) at points on its sides, the circle lies entirely inside the quadrilateral. The internal angle bisectors of the quadrilateral pass through \\( O \\), the center of the circle.\n\nLet the angles at \\( A, B, C, D \\) be \\( \\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta \\) respectively. We seek to find the sum of the angles \\( \\angle AOB + \\angle COD \\) and \\( \\angle BOC + \\angle DOA \\).\n\nThe internal angles of a quadrilateral sum up to \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n\nWe can start by splitting the angles around \\( O \\):\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle AOB & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{2}, \\\\\n\\angle COD & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\gamma + \\delta}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n \nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\angle AOB + \\angle COD = (180^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{2}) + (180^\\circ - \\frac{\\gamma + \\delta}{2}) \n= 360^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta}{2} \n= 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ \n= 180^\\circ. \n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}, \\\\\n\\angle DOA & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\delta + \\alpha}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n \nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC + \\angle DOA &= (180^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}) + (180^\\circ - \\frac{\\delta + \\alpha}{2}) \\\\\n&= 360^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma + \\delta + \\alpha}{2} \\\\\n&= 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ \\\\\n&= 180^\\circ. \n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n#### II. Two Tangent Points Lie Outside the Segments of the Quadrilateral:\n\nLet's analyze the two cases:\n- The pairs of tangent lines are adjacent.\n- The pairs of tangent lines are opposite.\n\n**Case 1: Adjacent sides are tangent**\n\nFor \\( ABCD \\) with the circle tangential to \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\) at points outside the segments:\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle AOB & = \\frac{180^\\circ - (\\alpha + \\beta)}{2}, \\\\\n\\angle COD & = \\frac{360^\\circ - (\\gamma + \\delta)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n \nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle AOB + \\angle COD &= \\frac{180^\\circ - \\alpha - \\beta}{2} + \\frac{360^\\circ - \\gamma - \\delta}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{180^\\circ + \\gamma + \\delta - \\alpha - \\beta}{2} \\\\\n&= 180^\\circ.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}, \\\\\n\\angle DOA & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\delta + \\alpha}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n \nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC + \\angle DOA &= 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2} + 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\delta + \\alpha}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^\\circ - \\beta - \\gamma - \\delta - \\alpha}{2} \\\\\n&= 180^\\circ.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n**Case 2: Opposite sides are tangent**\n\nIf the tangent points are opposite (e.g., touching \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\)):\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle AOB & = \\frac{180^\\circ - (\\alpha + \\gamma)}{2}, \\\\\n\\angle COD & = \\frac{360^\\circ - (\\delta + \\beta)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle AOB + \\angle COD &= \\frac{180^\\circ - \\alpha - \\gamma}{2} + \\frac{360^\\circ - \\delta - \\beta}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{180^\\circ + \\delta + \\beta - \\alpha - \\gamma}{2} \\\\\n&= 180^\\circ.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\delta}{2}, \\\\\n\\angle DOA & = 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\gamma + \\alpha}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC + \\angle DOA &= 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\beta + \\delta}{2} + 180^\\circ - \\frac{\\gamma + \\alpha}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^\\circ - \\beta - \\delta - \\gamma - \\alpha}{2} \\\\\n&= 180^\\circ.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n#### III. All Four Tangent Points Lie Outside the Segments of the Quadrilateral:\n\nIn this case, assuming the tangent points do not lie on the sides:\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle AOB & = 180^\\circ - \\alpha - \\beta, \\\\\n\\angle COD & = 180^\\circ - \\delta - \\gamma.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\angle AOB + \\angle COD = 180^\\circ - \\alpha - \\beta + 180^\\circ - \\gamma - \\delta = 360^\\circ - (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta) = 180^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC & = 180^\\circ - \\beta - \\gamma, \\\\\n\\angle DOA & = 180^\\circ - \\delta - \\alpha.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ \n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BOC + \\angle DOA &= 180^\\circ - \\beta - \\gamma + 180^\\circ - \\delta - \\alpha \\\\\n&= 360^\\circ - (\\beta + \\gamma + \\delta + \\alpha) \\\\\n&= 180^\\circ.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nIn all cases, the sums of the angles \\( \\angle AOB + \\angle COD \\) and \\( \\angle BOC + \\angle DOA \\) are both \\( 180^\\circ \\).\n\n\\[ \n\\boxed{180^\\circ}\n\\]", "domain": "