)/AC? Or is it (AB + BC)/AC? Let me check.\n\nActually, the formula for the ratio in which the incenter divides the angle bisector is (AB + BC)/AC. Wait, let me confirm. If we have angle bisector from vertex B to side AC, meeting at L, then the incenter I divides BL in the ratio BI:IL = (AB + BC)/AC. Yes, that seems right. Because the incenter divides the angle bisector in the ratio of the sum of the two adjacent sides to the opposite side. So, BI/IL = (AB + BC)/AC.\n\nBut in the problem, the ratio is given as 4:3. Therefore, (AB + BC)/AC = 4/3. Since BC is given as 28, so substituting BC = 28, we have (AB + 28)/AC = 4/3. Let me note that down:\n\n(AB + 28)/AC = 4/3 \u21d2 3(AB + 28) = 4AC \u21d2 3AB + 84 = 4AC \u21d2 3AB = 4AC - 84 \u21d2 AB = (4AC - 84)/3. Let's keep this as equation (1).\n\nNow, we also know the inradius r = 12. The formula for the inradius is r = A/s, where A is the area and s is the semiperimeter. So, A = r*s = 12s.\n\nThe semiperimeter s = (AB + BC + AC)/2 = (AB + 28 + AC)/2. Let's denote AB as c, BC as a = 28, and AC as b. Wait, but usually, sides are denoted as a opposite to A, b opposite to B, and c opposite to C. Let me check. If BC is 28, then BC is side a (opposite to angle A), but maybe the notation can vary. Hmm, maybe it's better to assign variables accordingly.\n\nLet me set AB = c, BC = a = 28, and AC = b. Then the semiperimeter s = (a + b + c)/2 = (28 + b + c)/2.\n\nWe also have from the ratio above: (c + 28)/b = 4/3 \u21d2 c = (4b/3) - 28. Wait, equation (1) is c = (4b - 84)/3, which is the same as c = (4/3)b - 28. So, AB = (4/3)b - 28. So, if we can express AB in terms of AC (which is b), then maybe we can relate the sides.\n\nOur goal is to find the circumradius R. For that, we need the sides a, b, c and the area A. Since we know a = 28, if we can find b and c, then we can compute A via Heron's formula (since we know the inradius is 12, which gives A = 12s). Alternatively, since we have two expressions involving A, maybe we can set them equal.\n\nBut let's see. Let's write down the area in two ways: one using Heron's formula, and one using the inradius. So:\n\nA = sqrt[s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)] (Heron's formula)\n\nAnd A = r*s = 12s.\n\nTherefore, 12s = sqrt[s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)]\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\n144s\u00b2 = s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)\n\nDivide both sides by s (assuming s \u2260 0, which it isn't):\n\n144s = (s - a)(s - b)(s - c)\n\nSo, 144s = (s - 28)(s - b)(s - c)\n\nBut we have variables here. Let's see if we can express c in terms of b. From earlier, we have c = (4b - 84)/3. Let's substitute that.\n\nSo, c = (4b - 84)/3. Let's write s in terms of b and c:\n\ns = (28 + b + c)/2 = (28 + b + (4b - 84)/3)/2. Let's compute this:\n\nFirst, express all terms with denominator 3:\n\n= [28*(3/3) + b*(3/3) + (4b - 84)/3]/2\n\n= [ (84 + 3b + 4b - 84) / 3 ] / 2\n\n= [ (7b) / 3 ] / 2\n\n= (7b)/(3*2) = 7b/6.\n\nSo, s = 7b/6.\n\nWait, that's interesting. The semiperimeter s is (7b)/6. Therefore, s = (7b)/6. Let's note that.\n\nNow, let's substitute s into the equation 144s = (s - a)(s - b)(s - c). First, compute each term:\n\ns - a = (7b/6) - 28 = (7b - 168)/6\n\ns - b = (7b/6) - b = (7b - 6b)/6 = b/6\n\ns - c = (7b/6) - c. But c = (4b - 84)/3, so:\n\ns - c = (7b/6) - (4b - 84)/3 = Convert to sixths:\n\n= (7b/6) - (8b - 168)/6 = (7b - 8b + 168)/6 = (-b + 168)/6\n\nTherefore, (s - a)(s - b)(s - c) = [(7b - 168)/6] * [b/6] * [(-b + 168)/6]\n\nMultiply these together:\n\n= [(7b - 168)(b)(-b + 168)] / (6*6*6) = [ (7b - 168)(b)(168 - b) ] / 216\n\nNote that (7b - 168) = 7(b - 24), and (168 - b) = -(b - 168). Let me see:\n\nBut let's compute the numerator:\n\nFirst, expand (7b - 168)(168 - b):\n\nLet me compute (7b - 168)(168 - b) = 7b*168 - 7b\u00b2 - 168*168 + 168b = (7b*168 + 168b) - 7b\u00b2 - 168\u00b2\n\n= 168b(7 + 1) - 7b\u00b2 - 168\u00b2 = 168b*8 - 7b\u00b2 - 168\u00b2 = 1344b - 7b\u00b2 - 28224\n\nThen multiply by b:\n\n= b*(1344b - 7b\u00b2 - 28224) = 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28224b\n\nSo, the numerator is 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28224b\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n144s = [1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28224b]/216\n\nBut s = 7b/6, so 144*(7b/6) = [1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28224b]/216\n\nCompute the left side:\n\n144*(7b/6) = (144/6)*7b = 24*7b = 168b\n\nSo:\n\n168b = [1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28224b]/216\n\nMultiply both sides by 216:\n\n168b * 216 = 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28224b\n\nCompute 168*216:\n\n168*200 = 33,600\n\n168*16 = 2,688\n\nTotal: 33,600 + 2,688 = 36,288\n\nSo, 36,288b = 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28,224b\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n7b\u00b3 - 1344b\u00b2 + (28,224b + 36,288b) = 0\n\nWait, wait:\n\nWait, original equation after multiplying by 216:\n\n36,288b = 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28,224b\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n7b\u00b3 - 1344b\u00b2 + 28,224b + 36,288b = 0\n\nWait, no, actually:\n\n36,288b - 1344b\u00b2 + 7b\u00b3 + 28,224b = 0\n\nWait, that's confusing. Let's rearrange properly.\n\nStarting from:\n\n36,288b = 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28,224b\n\nSubtract all terms from both sides:\n\n0 = 1344b\u00b2 - 7b\u00b3 - 28,224b - 36,288b\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n0 = -7b\u00b3 + 1344b\u00b2 - (28,224 + 36,288)b\n\nCalculate 28,224 + 36,288:\n\n28,224 + 36,288 = 64,512\n\nSo:\n\n0 = -7b\u00b3 + 1344b\u00b2 - 64,512b\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n0 = 7b\u00b3 - 1344b\u00b2 + 64,512b\n\nFactor out a common term. Let's see:\n\n7b\u00b3 - 1344b\u00b2 + 64,512b = b(7b\u00b2 - 1344b + 64,512)\n\nFactor out 7 from the quadratic:\n\n= 7b(b\u00b2 - (1344/7)b + 64,512/7)\n\nCalculate 1344/7 = 192\n\n64,512 /7 \u2248 64,512 \u00f77. 7*9,000 = 63,000, so 64,512 - 63,000 = 1,512. 1,512/7 = 216. So total is 9,000 + 216 = 9,216.\n\nTherefore:\n\n= 7b(b\u00b2 - 192b + 9,216)\n\nNow, factor the quadratic:\n\nb\u00b2 - 192b + 9,216. Let's check discriminant: 192\u00b2 - 4*1*9,216.\n\n192\u00b2 = (200 - 8)\u00b2 = 200\u00b2 - 2*200*8 + 8\u00b2 = 40,000 - 3,200 + 64 = 36,864\n\n4*1*9,216 = 36,864\n\nSo discriminant = 36,864 - 36,864 = 0. So it's a perfect square.\n\nThus, the quadratic factors as (b - 96)^2\n\nTherefore, overall:\n\n7b(b - 96)^2 = 0\n\nSolutions are b = 0 (discarded, since side length can't be zero) and b = 96 (double root). So, the only valid solution is b = 96. Therefore, AC = 96.\n\nNow, using equation (1), c = (4b - 84)/3 = (4*96 - 84)/3 = (384 - 84)/3 = 300/3 = 100. So, AB = 100.\n\nSo, sides are BC = a = 28, AC = b = 96, AB = c = 100.\n\nNow, check the semiperimeter s = (28 + 96 + 100)/2 = (224)/2 = 112. Wait, but earlier we had s = 7b/6 = 7*96/6 = 7*16 = 112. That matches, good.\n\nNow, let's check the inradius. The area A = r*s = 12*112 = 1,344.\n\nAlternatively, using Heron's formula: A = sqrt[s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)] = sqrt[112*(112 - 28)*(112 - 96)*(112 - 100)].\n\nCalculate each term:\n\ns - a = 112 - 28 = 84\n\ns - b = 112 - 96 = 16\n\ns - c = 112 - 100 = 12\n\nTherefore, A = sqrt[112*84*16*12]\n\nCompute this:\n\nFirst, multiply 112 and 84:\n\n112*84: 100*84=8,400; 12*84=1,008; total=8,400+1,008=9,408\n\nThen multiply 16 and 12: 16*12=192\n\nSo, total product: 9,408*192\n\nLet me compute 9,408*200 = 1,881,600, subtract 9,408*8 = 75,264, so 1,881,600 - 75,264 = 1,806,336\n\nTherefore, A = sqrt[1,806,336]. Let's calculate sqrt(1,806,336).\n\nNote that 1,344\u00b2 = (1,300 + 44)\u00b2 = 1,300\u00b2 + 2*1,300*44 + 44\u00b2 = 1,690,000 + 114,400 + 1,936 = 1,690,000 + 114,400 = 1,804,400 + 1,936 = 1,806,336. Perfect! So A = 1,344, which matches the inradius formula. Good.\n\nNow, we need to find the circumradius R. The formula is R = (a*b*c)/(4*A). Let's plug in the values:\n\na = 28, b = 96, c = 100, A = 1,344.\n\nCompute numerator: 28*96*100\n\nFirst, 28*96: 20*96=1,920; 8*96=768; total=1,920+768=2,688\n\nThen, 2,688*100 = 268,800\n\nDenominator: 4*A = 4*1,344 = 5,376\n\nSo, R = 268,800 / 5,376\n\nSimplify:\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 16: 268,800 \u00f716=16,800; 5,376 \u00f716=336\n\nSo, 16,800 / 336\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 48: 16,800 \u00f748=350; 336 \u00f748=7\n\nSo, 350 /7 = 50.\n\nTherefore, R = 50.\n\nWait, that's straightforward. Let me confirm:\n\n28*96 = 2,688\n\n2,688*100 = 268,800\n\n4*A = 5,376\n\n268,800 /5,376: Let's divide 268,800 by 5,376:\n\n5,376 x 50 = 268,800. Exactly. So R = 50.\n\nSo the circumradius is 50. Let me just check if all steps make sense and there are no calculation errors.\n\nWe found AC = 96, AB = 100, semiperimeter 112, area 1,344. Using the formula R = (a*b*c)/(4*A), got 50. Seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, another formula for circumradius is R = a/(2 sin A). But since we have all sides, the formula used is more straightforward.\n\nHence, the answer should be 50.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{50}", "deepseek_solution": "Given triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(BC = 28\\), the angle bisector \\(BL\\) is divided by the incenter \\(I\\) in the ratio \\(4:3\\) from vertex \\(B\\). The inradius \\(r\\) is 12, and we need to find the circumradius \\(R\\).\n\n1. **Ratio of Division by Incenter**:\n   The incenter divides the angle bisector \\(BL\\) in the ratio \\((AB + BC)/AC = 4/3\\). Given \\(BC = 28\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AB + 28}{AC} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(AB\\):\n   \\[\n   3(AB + 28) = 4AC \\implies 3AB + 84 = 4AC \\implies AB = \\frac{4AC - 84}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Semiperimeter and Inradius**:\n   The inradius \\(r = 12\\) gives the area \\(A = r \\cdot s\\), where \\(s\\) is the semiperimeter. Let \\(AC = b\\) and \\(AB = c\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{28 + b + c}{2}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(c = \\frac{4b - 84}{3}\\) into \\(s\\):\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{28 + b + \\frac{4b - 84}{3}}{2} = \\frac{7b}{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Heron's Formula**:\n   The area \\(A\\) is also given by Heron's formula:\n   \\[\n   A = \\sqrt{s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(s = \\frac{7b}{6}\\), \\(a = 28\\), and solving for \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   144s = (s - a)(s - b)(s - c)\n   \\]\n   Solving this equation, we find \\(b = 96\\). Substituting back, we get \\(AB = 100\\) and \\(s = 112\\).\n\n4. **Area Calculation**:\n   Using the inradius formula:\n   \\[\n   A = 12 \\cdot 112 = 1344\n   \\]\n\n5. **Circumradius Calculation**:\n   Using the formula \\(R = \\frac{abc}{4A}\\) with \\(a = 28\\), \\(b = 96\\), \\(c = 100\\), and \\(A = 1344\\):\n   \\[\n   R = \\frac{28 \\cdot 96 \\cdot 100}{4 \\cdot 1344} = \\frac{268800}{5376} = 50\n   \\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circumscribed circle around triangle \\(ABC\\) is \\(\\boxed{50}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. We start with understanding the given triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\). The side \\( BC \\) is given as 28 units. \n2. The angle bisector \\( BL \\) is divided into the ratio \\( 4:3 \\) by the point of intersection with the other bisectors of the triangle, considering from the vertex \\( B \\).\n3. Let's identify \\( I \\) as the incenter of \\( \\triangle ABC \\), which is the point of intersection of all angle bisectors of the triangle (\\( BL \\), \\( CI \\), and the angle bisector from \\( A \\)).\n4. Since \\( CI \\) is an angle bisector, in \\( \\triangle BLC \\), using the angle bisector theorem, we have: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{BC}{CL} = \\frac{BI}{IL} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   CL = \\frac{3 \\cdot BC}{4} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 28}{4} = 21 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n5. Next, we use the formula for calculating the area of a triangle, \\( \\Delta ABC \\), through the semi-perimeter \\( s \\) and the inradius \\( r \\). Understand that:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area } = \\Delta = r \\cdot s \n   \\]\n6. Let\u2019s compute the total area \\((\\Delta_{BLC})\\) in terms of sine for angle \\( \\angle C \\):\n   \\[\n   BC \\cdot CL \\cdot \\sin \\angle C = 2 \\Delta_{BLC} = 2 \\Delta_{BIC} + 2 \\Delta_{CIL}\n   \\]\n   where \\( r = 12 \\) units.\n7. We know:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_{BIC} + \\Delta_{CIL} = \\Delta_{ABC}\n   \\]\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   28 \\cdot 21 \\cdot \\sin \\angle C = (28+21) \\cdot 12\n   \\]\n   Simplifying,\n   \\[\n   588 \\cdot \\sin \\angle C = 49 \\cdot 12 = 588\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\angle C = 1 \\implies \\angle C = 90^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n\n8. Given that \\( \\angle C = 90^{\\circ} \\), \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is a right triangle. Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   AB^2 = AC^2 + BC^2 \n   \\]\n\n9. Considering the side ratios from the angle bisector theorem in \\( \\triangle ALB \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BA}{AL} = \\frac{BI}{IL} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n   Let \\( AL = 3x \\) and \\( AB = 4x \\).\n\n10. Using the Pythagorean theorem on the sides of the triangle:\n    \\[\n    BC^2 + (AL + CL)^2 = AB^2\n    \\]\n    Substituting for the side lengths:\n    \\[\n    28^2 + (21 + 3x)^2 = (4x)^2\n    784 + (21 + 3x)^2 = 16x^2\n    \\]\n\n11. Solving the quadratic equation:\n    \\[\n    784 + 441 + 126x + 9x^2 = 16x^2\n    1225 + 126x + 9x^2 = 16x^2\n    1225 + 126x = 7x^2\n    7x = 25\n    x = 25\n    \\]\n\n12. Finally, the radius of the circumscribed circle \\( R \\) is:\n    \\[\n    R = \\frac{AB}{2} = \\frac{4x}{2} = \\frac{4 \\cdot 25}{2} = 50\n    \\]\n\nConclusion. \n\\[\n\\boxed{50}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Vasya remembers that his friend Petya lives on Kurchatovskaya street in building number 8, but he forgot the apartment number. When asked for clarification, Petya replied: \"The number of my apartment is a three-digit number. If you rearrange its digits, you get five other three-digit numbers. The sum of these five numbers is exactly 2017.\" Help Vasya remember Petya's apartment number.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. So, Petya's apartment number is a three-digit number. When you rearrange its digits, you get five other three-digit numbers, and the sum of those five numbers is 2017. We need to find the original number. Hmm, okay. Let's start by understanding the problem clearly.\n\nFirst, let's note that the original number is a three-digit number. Let's denote it as ABC, where A, B, and C are digits, and A is not zero (since it's a three-digit number). When we rearrange the digits, we get all possible permutations of A, B, and C, except the original one. So, the five other numbers would be permutations like ACB, BAC, BCA, CAB, CBA, etc. However, if any of the digits are the same, some permutations would repeat, but the problem statement mentions \"five other three-digit numbers,\" which suggests that all three digits are distinct. Because if there were a repeated digit, some permutations would result in the same number, leading to fewer than five distinct permutations. Therefore, we can conclude that A, B, and C are all distinct digits.\n\nSo, the problem reduces to finding a three-digit number ABC with distinct digits such that the sum of the five other permutations of its digits equals 2017. Let's formalize this.\n\nLet\u2019s denote the original number as N = 100A + 10B + C. The five permutations would then be:\n\n1. 100A + 10C + B\n2. 100B + 10A + C\n3. 100B + 10C + A\n4. 100C + 10A + B\n5. 100C + 10B + A\n\nAdding these five numbers together should give 2017. Let's compute the sum of these five permutations.\n\nFirst, let's expand each term:\n\n1. 100A + 10C + B\n2. 100B + 10A + C\n3. 100B + 10C + A\n4. 100C + 10A + B\n5. 100C + 10B + A\n\nNow, sum all these:\n\nSum = (100A + 10C + B) + (100B + 10A + C) + (100B + 10C + A) + (100C + 10A + B) + (100C + 10B + A)\n\nLet's group the terms by their place values (hundreds, tens, ones):\n\nHundreds place:\n- From the first term: 100A\n- From the second term: 100B\n- From the third term: 100B\n- From the fourth term: 100C\n- From the fifth term: 100C\n\nTotal hundreds: 100A + 100B + 100B + 100C + 100C = 100A + 200B + 200C\n\nTens place:\n- From the first term: 10C\n- From the second term: 10A\n- From the third term: 10C\n- From the fourth term: 10A\n- From the fifth term: 10B\n\nTotal tens: 10C + 10A + 10C + 10A + 10B = 20A + 10B + 20C\n\nOnes place:\n- From the first term: B\n- From the second term: C\n- From the third term: A\n- From the fourth term: B\n- From the fifth term: A\n\nTotal ones: B + C + A + B + A = 2A + 2B + C\n\nSo the total sum is:\n\nSum = (100A + 200B + 200C) + (20A + 10B + 20C) + (2A + 2B + C)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFor hundreds (A, B, C coefficients):\n\n100A + 20A + 2A = 122A\n200B + 10B + 2B = 212B\n200C + 20C + C = 221C\n\nTherefore, Sum = 122A + 212B + 221C\n\nAccording to the problem, this sum is equal to 2017. So:\n\n122A + 212B + 221C = 2017\n\nBut wait, let's check the arithmetic here. Wait, when we combined the hundreds, tens, and ones:\n\nWait, the hundreds place contributions were 100A + 200B + 200C. The tens place contributions were 20A + 10B + 20C, which is 20A + 10B + 20C. The ones place was 2A + 2B + C. So when combining all together:\n\nHundreds: 100A + 200B + 200C\n\nTens: 20A + 10B + 20C (which is 20A +10B +20C) \u2192 this is equivalent to 20A +10B +20C = 20A +10B +20C\n\nOnes: 2A + 2B + C\n\nAdding them together:\n\n(100A + 20A + 2A) + (200B + 10B + 2B) + (200C + 20C + C) =\n\n122A + 212B + 221C = 2017\n\nYes, that seems correct. So our equation is:\n\n122A + 212B + 221C = 2017\n\nWe need to find digits A, B, C (distinct, each from 0-9, A \u2260 0) such that this equation holds.\n\nThis equation is crucial. Let's think about how to approach solving it.\n\nFirst, note that A, B, C are digits between 0 and 9, with A \u2265 1. So possible values for A: 1-9, B and C: 0-9, all distinct.\n\nGiven that 122A + 212B + 221C = 2017.\n\nThis seems a bit complicated, but maybe we can find some modular relationships or look for possible values.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a smarter way to approach the problem. Let me recall that when you have all permutations of digits, the sum can be related to the sum of digits. Wait, but in this problem, we are not considering all permutations, only five of them, excluding the original number. Let's think about if we consider all six permutations, then their sum would be a certain value, and here we have the sum of five of them.\n\nIn general, for three distinct digits A, B, C, each digit appears in each place (hundreds, tens, ones) exactly 2 times in all six permutations. Therefore, the sum of all six permutations would be:\n\nSum_all = 222*(A + B + C)\n\nBecause each digit appears twice in hundreds place (sum 200*(A+B+C)), twice in tens (sum 20*(A+B+C)), twice in ones (sum 2*(A+B+C)), so total 222*(A+B+C).\n\nBut in our problem, the sum of five permutations is 2017. Therefore, the sum of all six permutations would be 2017 + N, where N is the original apartment number. So:\n\n2017 + N = 222*(A + B + C)\n\nTherefore, N = 222*(A + B + C) - 2017\n\nSince N is a three-digit number, 100 \u2264 N \u2264 999.\n\nSo 222*(A + B + C) = 2017 + N\n\nTherefore, 222*(A + B + C) must be between 2017 + 100 = 2117 and 2017 + 999 = 3016.\n\nCompute 2117 / 222 \u2248 9.536, so the lower bound of (A+B+C) is 10 (since it must be integer). Similarly, 3016 / 222 \u2248 13.58, so upper bound is 13. Thus, A + B + C can be 10, 11, 12, or 13.\n\nTherefore, possible values for (A + B + C) are 10, 11, 12, 13.\n\nThen, N = 222*S - 2017, where S = A + B + C, and S \u2208 {10,11,12,13}\n\nCompute N for each S:\n\nFor S=10: N = 222*10 - 2017 = 2220 - 2017 = 203\n\nBut 203 is a three-digit number. However, the digits of 203 are 2, 0, 3. Their sum is 2+0+3=5, which is not 10. Contradiction. Therefore, S=10 is invalid.\n\nFor S=11: N = 222*11 - 2017 = 2442 - 2017 = 425\n\nCheck if sum of digits 4+2+5=11. Yes, 4+2+5=11. So possible. Now, check if the sum of the five permutations is 2017.\n\nWait, but let's check if the original number is 425. Then, the permutations (excluding 425) are 452, 245, 254, 524, 542. Let's sum them:\n\n452 + 245 = 697\n\n254 + 524 = 778\n\n542 + 697 (wait, wait, actually, need to sum all five:\n\nWait, the original number is 425. The five permutations are:\n\n1. 452\n\n2. 245\n\n3. 254\n\n4. 524\n\n5. 542\n\nLet's compute each:\n\n452\n\n245\n\n254\n\n524\n\n542\n\nAdd them up step by step:\n\n452 + 245 = 697\n\n697 + 254 = 951\n\n951 + 524 = 1475\n\n1475 + 542 = 2017\n\nWow! That adds up exactly to 2017. So the apartment number is 425. Wait, but let's check the digits: 4,2,5. All distinct. Yes. And the sum 4+2+5=11, which matches S=11.\n\nSo, this seems to fit. However, let's check the other possibilities for thoroughness.\n\nFor S=12: N = 222*12 - 2017 = 2664 - 2017 = 647\n\nCheck digits: 6+4+7=17 \u226012. Contradiction. So invalid.\n\nWait, sum S=12, but 6+4+7=17. Not 12. Hence invalid.\n\nNext, S=13: N = 222*13 - 2017 = 2886 - 2017 = 869\n\nCheck digits: 8+6+9=23 \u226013. Again contradiction. So invalid.\n\nTherefore, the only valid case is S=11, N=425. Which seems to check out.\n\nBut let's verify again. Original number 425. The five permutations are:\n\n- 452, 245, 254, 524, 542\n\nSum them:\n\n452 + 245 = 697\n\n697 + 254 = 951\n\n951 + 524 = 1475\n\n1475 + 542 = 2017\n\nYes, correct.\n\nTherefore, the apartment number is 425.\n\nWait, but let's make sure there are no other possible numbers. Let's check S=10, S=12, S=13 again.\n\nFor S=10: N=203. Digits sum 2+0+3=5\u226010. Invalid.\n\nFor S=12: N=647. Sum 6+4+7=17\u226012. Invalid.\n\nFor S=13: N=869. Sum 8+6+9=23\u226013. Invalid.\n\nThus, only S=11 gives a valid number with digits summing to 11.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 425. But wait, let's check another thing. Is there any other three-digit number with digits summing to 11 where the sum of its five permutations is 2017?\n\nSuppose there is another number with digits summing to 11. Let's see.\n\nFor example, let's take another number with digits summing to 11. For example, 632. Sum 6+3+2=11. Let's check the sum of permutations.\n\nOriginal number 632. Exclude it, permutations are:\n\n632 excluded, so permutations are:\n\n623, 362, 326, 263, 236.\n\nSum them:\n\n623 + 362 = 985\n\n985 + 326 = 1311\n\n1311 + 263 = 1574\n\n1574 + 236 = 1810\n\nWhich is not 2017. So this doesn't work.\n\nAnother example: 341. Sum 3+4+1=8. Not 11.\n\nWait, another example: 506. Sum 5+0+6=11. Let's check permutations.\n\nOriginal number 506. Exclude it, permutations are:\n\n560, 056 (invalid, since it's two-digit), 065 (invalid), 605, 650, 065. Wait, but since leading zeros are not allowed, the valid permutations are 560, 605, 650. But wait, digits are 5,0,6. So permutations:\n\n506 (original), 560, 605, 650, 056 (invalid), 065 (invalid). So the five other numbers are only three valid numbers: 560, 605, 650. But the problem states that there are five other three-digit numbers. Therefore, the digits must not include a zero because if there is a zero, some permutations would result in numbers with leading zeros, which are not valid three-digit numbers. Wait, but in our original problem, the five other numbers are all three-digit, which implies that all permutations (excluding the original) must be three-digit numbers. Therefore, the digits cannot contain zero. Because if one of the digits is zero, then some permutations would start with zero, making them two-digit numbers, which are invalid. Hence, the digits A, B, C must all be non-zero. Therefore, in our previous consideration, the digits must be from 1-9, all distinct, and non-zero. Therefore, the previous example with 506 is invalid because it includes a zero, leading to invalid permutations. Hence, the original number must have all digits non-zero. Therefore, A, B, C are from 1-9, distinct.\n\nWait, but wait, the problem statement says \"five other three-digit numbers\". So when you rearrange the digits, you get five other three-digit numbers. Therefore, none of the permutations can have leading zeros, which implies that none of the digits can be zero. Because if a digit is zero, then when you permute, putting zero in the hundreds place would make it a two-digit number, which is invalid. Therefore, all digits must be non-zero.\n\nTherefore, the digits A, B, C are from 1-9, distinct. Therefore, in our previous calculation for S=11, N=425 includes a digit 0? Wait no, 425 has digits 4,2,5. None are zero. So that's okay. Wait, but in the number 425, digits are 4,2,5: all non-zero. So permutations are all valid three-digit numbers. Therefore, that's acceptable.\n\nWait, but when we considered S=11, we had N=425. Sum of digits 4+2+5=11, which is correct. The permutations are all three-digit numbers with digits 4,2,5, none including zero, so no leading zeros. Therefore, valid.\n\nBut when we considered S=10, we had N=203, which includes a zero, but that was invalid because sum didn't match. Similarly, S=12 and S=13 led to numbers with digits summing higher. So 425 seems the only valid one.\n\nBut to be thorough, let's check another number with digits summing to 11, all non-zero. For example, 317: 3+1+7=11. Let's compute the sum of permutations.\n\nOriginal number 317. Permutations (excluding 317):\n\n371, 137, 173, 731, 713.\n\nSum them:\n\n371 + 137 = 508\n\n508 + 173 = 681\n\n681 + 731 = 1412\n\n1412 + 713 = 2125\n\nWhich is more than 2017. So not equal.\n\nAnother example: 218. Sum 2+1+8=11.\n\nPermutations: 281, 128, 182, 812, 821.\n\nSum:\n\n281 + 128 = 409\n\n409 + 182 = 591\n\n591 + 812 = 1403\n\n1403 + 821 = 2224. Again, exceeds 2017.\n\nAnother example: 152. Sum 1+5+2=8. Not 11.\n\nWait, let's find all three-digit numbers with distinct digits, no zeros, and digits summing to 11. Let's list them.\n\nPossible combinations of digits (A,B,C) where A+B+C=11, all digits from 1-9, distinct.\n\nPossible triplets:\n\n1,2,8 (sum 11)\n\n1,3,7\n\n1,4,6\n\n1,5,5 (invalid, duplicate)\n\n2,3,6\n\n2,4,5\n\n3,4,4 (invalid)\n\nSo possible triplets:\n\n(1,2,8), (1,3,7), (1,4,6), (2,3,6), (2,4,5)\n\nEach of these can be arranged into 6 permutations. The original number is one, so the other five permutations are considered. For each triplet, we can compute the sum of the five permutations and check if it's 2017.\n\nWe already checked 425 (digits 4,2,5 which is triplet (2,4,5)) and it worked. Let's check others.\n\nFirst, triplet (1,2,8). Let's take the number 128. The permutations excluding 128 are 182, 218, 281, 812, 821. Sum them:\n\n182 + 218 = 400\n\n400 + 281 = 681\n\n681 + 812 = 1493\n\n1493 + 821 = 2314 \u22602017.\n\nNext, triplet (1,3,7). Take number 137. Permutations: 173, 317, 371, 713, 731. Sum:\n\n173 + 317 = 490\n\n490 + 371 = 861\n\n861 + 713 = 1574\n\n1574 + 731 = 2305 \u22602017.\n\nTriplet (1,4,6). Take number 146. Permutations: 164, 416, 461, 614, 641. Sum:\n\n164 + 416 = 580\n\n580 + 461 = 1041\n\n1041 + 614 = 1655\n\n1655 + 641 = 2296 \u22602017.\n\nTriplet (2,3,6). Take number 236. Permutations: 263, 326, 362, 623, 632. Sum:\n\n263 + 326 = 589\n\n589 + 362 = 951\n\n951 + 623 = 1574\n\n1574 + 632 = 2206 \u22602017.\n\nTriplet (2,4,5). Take number 245. Permutations: 254, 425, 452, 524, 542. Sum:\n\n254 + 425 = 679\n\n679 + 452 = 1131\n\n1131 + 524 = 1655\n\n1655 + 542 = 2197. Wait, but this contradicts our earlier calculation where 425 was the original number and the sum was 2017. Wait, no. Wait, if the original number is 425, then the permutations are 452, 245, 254, 524, 542. Let's sum them again:\n\n452 + 245 = 697\n\n697 + 254 = 951\n\n951 + 524 = 1475\n\n1475 + 542 = 2017\n\nYes, that's correct. So when the original number is 425, the permutations are different than when the original number is 245. Therefore, for the triplet (2,4,5), if we take different original numbers, the sum of permutations changes.\n\nLet's verify for original number 254. Then permutations would be 245, 425, 452, 524, 542. Sum is the same as before: 245 + 425 + 452 + 524 + 542. Which is the same as when the original number was 425, just different order. The sum remains 2017. Therefore, regardless of which number in the permutation set you take as original, the sum of the other five will be 2017. Therefore, all numbers in the permutation set (2,4,5) would satisfy the condition. However, the problem states that Petya's apartment number is such that when you rearrange the digits, the five other numbers sum to 2017. Therefore, any of the permutations would technically satisfy the condition. However, since the apartment number is unique, but according to the problem, there is only one such number. However, according to our analysis, all permutations of (2,4,5) would satisfy the condition. But wait, let's check.\n\nWait, no. Wait, if the original number is 245, then the sum of the other five permutations is 2017. Similarly, if the original number is 254, the sum is also 2017. Therefore, all six permutations of (2,4,5) would have the property that the sum of the other five is 2017. But the problem says that Vasya remembers the building number as 8, but forgot the apartment number, and Petya replied with the clue. However, the problem states that the apartment number is a three-digit number, and when you rearrange its digits, you get five other three-digit numbers whose sum is 2017. So all permutations (except the original) sum to 2017. Therefore, any of the six permutations could be the answer. But since the apartment number is unique, there must be only one possible answer. This suggests that our earlier reasoning might have a flaw.\n\nWait, but in our case, when we derived N = 222*(A+B+C) - 2017, and found that only S=11 gives a valid number 425. But according to this, all permutations of (2,4,5) would satisfy the condition, but when we calculated for S=11, we got N=425. But if we take another permutation, like 524, then according to the formula:\n\nN = 222*(5+2+4) -2017 = 222*11 -2017=2442-2017=425. Wait, no. Wait, if the original number is 524, then S=5+2+4=11, so N=222*11 -2017=425. But the original number is 524, but according to the formula, N=425. That contradicts. Wait, no, wait. The formula is:\n\nSum_all = 222*(A+B+C) = sum of all six permutations.\n\nBut if the original number is 524, then sum of the other five permutations is 2017. Therefore, sum_all = 2017 + 524 = 2541.\n\nBut 222*(5+2+4)=222*11=2442, which is not 2541. Contradiction.\n\nWait, this suggests a mistake in our earlier reasoning. Wait, let's go back.\n\nEarlier, we said that sum_all = sum of all six permutations = 222*(A+B+C). Therefore, sum of five permutations = sum_all - N = 222*(A+B+C) - N = 2017.\n\nTherefore, rearranged: N = 222*(A+B+C) - 2017.\n\nBut in the case of original number 425, sum_all = 222*11=2442. Therefore, sum of five permutations = 2442 - 425 = 2017, which matches.\n\nSimilarly, if the original number were 524, then sum of five permutations would be 2442 - 524 = 1918 \u22602017. Therefore, only when the original number is 425, the sum is 2017. Therefore, our formula holds, and only the number 425 satisfies the condition. Wait, but why?\n\nWait, because sum_all is fixed for all permutations. For digits 2,4,5, sum_all is 222*(11)=2442. Therefore, sum of five permutations is 2442 - N. We need this sum to be 2017. Therefore, 2442 - N =2017 \u2192 N=2442-2017=425. Therefore, only the original number 425 gives the sum of five permutations as 2017. The other permutations, when considered as original numbers, would give a different sum. For example, if original number is 245, then sum of five permutations would be 2442 -245=2197\u22602017. Similarly, for 254: 2442-254=2188\u22602017. Therefore, only 425 satisfies the condition. Hence, the answer is 425.\n\nTherefore, the correct apartment number is 425.\n\nLet me verify this once again with the permutations:\n\nOriginal number: 425\n\nPermutations (excluding 425):\n\n452, 245, 254, 524, 542\n\nSum them:\n\n452 + 245 = 697\n\n697 + 254 = 951\n\n951 + 524 = 1475\n\n1475 + 542 = 2017\n\nYes, correct. And sum_all =2017+425=2442=222*11=222*(4+2+5). Correct.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is 425.", "deepseek_solution": "The apartment number is determined by analyzing the sum of permutations and applying the relationship between the sum of all permutations and the target sum. \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - Petya's apartment number is a three-digit number with distinct digits (since rearranging produces five other numbers).\n   - The sum of these five permutations is 2017.\n\n2. **Sum of All Permutations:**\n   - For three distinct digits \\(A, B, C\\), each digit appears twice in each place (hundreds, tens, ones) across all six permutations.\n   - Total sum of all six permutations: \\(222 \\times (A + B + C)\\).\n\n3. **Relating to the Given Sum:**\n   - Let \\(N\\) be the original number. The sum of the five other permutations is \\(2017\\).\n   - Thus, \\(222(A + B + C) - N = 2017\\) \u2192 \\(N = 222(A + B + C) - 2017\\).\n\n4. **Possible Values for \\(A + B + C\\):**\n   - \\(N\\) must be a three-digit number, so \\(100 \\leq 222(A + B + C) - 2017 \\leq 999\\).\n   - Solving, \\(A + B + C\\) must be between 10 and 13. Testing these:\n     - \\(S = 11\\) gives \\(N = 222 \\times 11 - 2017 = 425\\).\n\n5. **Verification:**\n   - Check digits of 425: Sum \\(4 + 2 + 5 = 11\\) (correct).\n   - Permutations (excluding 425): 452, 245, 254, 524, 542.\n   - Sum: \\(452 + 245 + 254 + 524 + 542 = 2017\\).\n\n**Answer:** Petya's apartment number is \\(\\boxed{425}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Denote the digits of the apartment number as \\( a, b, c \\) respectively. Given that rearranging these digits results in exactly five other three-digit numbers, we can write the equation describing the sum of these numbers:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{acb} + \\overline{bac} + \\overline{bca} + \\overline{cab} + \\overline{cba} = 2017\n   \\]\n\n2. Using the property of three-digit numbers, we can express each number \\(\\overline{xyz}\\) as:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{xyz} = 100x + 10y + z\n   \\]\n   Thus, we break down each term:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{acb} = 100a + 10c + b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\overline{bac} = 100b + 10a + c\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\overline{bca} = 100b + 10c + a\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\overline{cab} = 100c + 10a + b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\overline{cba} = 100c + 10b + a\n   \\]\n\n3. Sum these expressions:\n   \\[\n   (100a + 10c + b) + (100b + 10a + c) + (100b + 10c + a) + (100c + 10a + b) + (100c + 10b + a)\n   \\]\n\n4. Simplify by combining like terms:\n   \\[\n   100a + 100b + 100b + 100c + 100c + 30a + 20b + 30c + 3a + 2c + 3b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 100a + 200b + 200c + 30a + 20b + 30c + 3a + 2c + 3b\n   \\]\n   Collect like terms:\n   \\[\n   = (133a + 123b + 132c)\n   \\]\n\n5. Given this, simplify to:\n   \\[\n   100(a+b+c) + 10(a+c+a+b) + (b+c+a)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2017\n   \\]\n\n6. Including \\(\\overline{abc}\\), we add it to both sides:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{abc} = 222 \\cdot (a + b + c) - 2017\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 100a + 10b + c\n   \\]\n\n7. Solve for feasible values of \\(a+b+c\\):\n   \\[\n   222 \\cdot (a + b + c) = 100a + 10b + c + 2017\n   \\]\n\n8. Given possible scenarios:\n   \\[\n   3016 \\geq 222(a+b+c) \\geq 2017 + 100 \\quad \\text{(Adding 979)}\n   \\]\n   This provides feasible values as 10, 11, 12, or 13\n\n9. Case Analysis for possible sums \\(a+b+c\\) :\n\n   - \\(a+b+c = 10\\): \n     \\[\n     \\overline{abc} = 2033 - 2017 = 203 \\quad \\text{(Not possible as sum of digits is 5 not 10)}\n     \\]\n\n   - \\(a+b+c = 11\\):\n     \\[\n     \\overline{abc} = 2220 - 2017 = 425 \\quad \\text{(425 satisfies original condition)}\n     \\]\n\n   - \\(a+b+c = 12\\):\n     \\[\n     \\overline{abc} = 2664 - 2017 = 647 \\quad \\text{(Not possible as sum of digits is 17 not 12)}\n     \\]\n\n   - \\(a+b+c = 13\\):\n     \\[\n     \\overline{abc} = 2886 - 2017 = 869 \\quad \\text{(Not possible as sum of digits is 23 not 13)}\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{425}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Dima and Sergey were picking berries from a raspberry bush that had 900 berries. Sergey alternated his actions: he would put one berry in the basket, then eat the next one. Dima also alternated his act