triangle CEF \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{I \\text{ is the incenter of } \\triangle CEF}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Given that \\( BC \\) is the diameter of circle \\(\\Gamma\\) and the center of \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(O\\), we start by recognizing that \\( D \\) is the midpoint of arc \\( AB \\) (not containing \\( C \\)). This implies that \\( D \\) is equidistant from \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). \n\n2. Since \\( \\angle AOB \\) is between \\( 0^\\circ \\) and \\( 120^\\circ \\), the corresponding arc \\( AB \\) subtends an angle at the center equal to \\( 120^\\circ \\). Hence, the central angle formed by the arc endpoints is:\n   \\[\n   \\angle AOB = 120^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \n3. The midpoint \\( D \\) of arc \\( AB \\) (not containing \\( C \\)) also implies that:\n   \\[\n   \\angle DOA = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\angle AOB = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 120^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore, \\( DO \\) is parallel to \\( AC \\) based on the fact that \\( \\angle OAC = \\frac{1}{2} \\angle AOC = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 60^\\circ = 30^\\circ \\), forming a corresponding angle in parallel lines.\n\n5. Since \\( DA \\parallel OI \\) and \\( DO \\parallel AC \\), quadrilateral \\( ADOI \\) is a parallelogram. This implies that:\n   \\[\n   AI = OD = OE\n   \\]\n\n6. By construction, \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) are points where the perpendicular bisector of \\( OA \\) intersects the circle \\( \\Gamma \\). Thus, \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) are such points that are symmetrically opposite with respect to \\( OA \\).\n\n7. Quadrilateral \\( AEOF \\) being a rectangle, given that its diagonals \\( OE \\) and \\( AF \\) are equal (since \\( OE \\) and \\( AF \\) are radii of the same circle):\n   \\[\n   OE = AF\n   \\]\nTherefore, \n   \\[\n   AI = AF\n   \\]\n\n8. Considering the quadrilateral \\( AEOF \\) and the fact that \\( OA \\) is perpendicular to \\( EF \\), we know:\n   \\[\n   \\angle IEF = \\angle IFA - \\angle EFA\n   \\]\n\n9. We also know:\n   \\[\n   \\angle IFA = \\angle AIF \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle EFA = \\angle ECA\n   \\]\n\n10. Thus, the angle \\( \\angle IFE \\) can be expressed as:\n    \\[\n    \\angle IFE = \\angle AIF - \\angle ECA = \\angle AIF - \\angle ICF = \\angle IFC\n    \\]\n   \n11. This implies that \\( IF \\) bisects \\( \\angle EFC \\), confirming that point \\( I \\) is the incenter of triangle \\( CEF \\):\n    \\[\n    I \\text{ bisects } \\angle EFC\n    \\]\n    Thus, \\( I \\) is the incenter of \\( \\triangle CEF \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{ }\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), point \\( F \\) lies on side \\( AC \\) such that \\( FC = 2AF \\). If \\( G \\) is the midpoint of segment \\( BF \\) and \\( E \\) is the intersection point of the line passing through \\( A \\) and \\( G \\) with segment \\( BC \\), calculate the ratio \\( \\frac{EC}{BE} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to figure out this geometry problem. So, we have triangle ABC. Point F is on AC such that FC is twice AF. Then G is the midpoint of BF. A line passes through A and G, intersecting BC at E. We need to find the ratio EC/BE. Hmm, okay, let me visualize this first.\n\nMaybe drawing a diagram would help. Let me sketch triangle ABC. Let me label the vertices A, B, C. Then, point F is on AC. Since FC = 2AF, that means AF is one-third of AC, and FC is two-thirds. So, if AC is divided into three equal parts, F is one part from A and two parts from C. Got it. Then, G is the midpoint of BF. So, BF is the segment connecting B and F, and G is halfway between them. Then, the line AG intersects BC at E. We need to find EC/BE.\n\nI think coordinate geometry might be a good approach here. Let me assign coordinates to the points to make it easier. Let's place point A at (0, 0) for simplicity. Let me let point C be at (3, 0) because FC = 2AF. If AC is 3 units, then AF is 1 unit, and FC is 2 units. So, if A is at (0,0), then F wou