e pairs of cities that need to be connected can be calculated using the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   For \\( n = 120 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{120}{2} = \\frac{120 \\cdot 119}{2} = 7140\n   \\]\n   Therefore, there are 7140 pairs of cities.\n\n2. **Condition for Airlines**:\n   - Each airline must offer flights in such a way that any two cities can be reachable:\n     - Direct flights between cities must form a connected graph (so you can travel from any city to any other city, possibly with layovers).\n\n3. **Graph Theory Insight**:\n   - In graph theory terms, we need a maximum number of edge-disjoint spanning trees\u2014where each tree is a network operated by one airline.\n   - A spanning tree in this context means that each airline's network must cover all 120 cities. The number of edges in each spanning tree with \\( n \\) vertices is \\( n-1 \\), which is \\( 119 \\) for \\( n = 120 \\).\n\n4. **Maximum Number of Airlines \\( k \\)**:\n   - If each airline covers a spanning tree, the minimum number of edges required (or flights) is \\((n-1) \\times k\\):\n   \\[\n   (n-1)k \\leq \\binom{n}{2}\n   \\]\n   For \\( n = 120 \\):\n   \\[\n   119k \\leq 7140\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( k \\):\n   \\[\n   k \\leq \\frac{7140}{119}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k \\leq 60\n   \\]\n\n5. **Achieving the Maximum \\( k \\)**:\n   - We next check if 60 airlines can indeed be constructed:\n     - Suppose we divide the cities into 2 groups, \\( \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_{60}\\} \\) and \\( \\{b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_{60}\\} \\).\n     - We can design the airline \\( k \\) to connect the cities in such a way that it forms spanning trees, ensuring all cities are reachable.\n\n     Each airline \\( k \\) would operate flights:\n     1. Between \\( a_k \\) and \\( b_k \\).\n     2. Between \\( a_k \\) and each \\( a_i \\) for \\( i < k \\).\n     3. Between \\( a_k \\) and each \\( b_i \\) for \\( i > k \\).\n     4. Between \\( b_k \\