nating), and the number of different bits after is maximized. Wait, but d_j is the number of same before plus different after. If we alternate, then for a 0 at position j, the number of same before is 0 (since previous is 1), and the number of different after is the number of 1s after j. For a 1 at position j, the number of same before is 0, and the number of different after is the number of 0s after j.\n\nBut since the sequence alternates, the number of different after would be roughly n - j. However, sum binom(d_j,2) would be sum binom(n - j, 2) for each j, which might be large. This might not be optimal.\n\nAlternatively, consider a sequence with all bits the same. Then, for each j, d_j is the number of same before plus different after. If all are 0s, then for each j, d_j = (j-1) + 0 = j-1. Therefore, sum binom(d_j,2) = sum_{j=1 to n} binom(j-1,2) = sum_{k=0 to n-1} binom(k,2) = binom(n,3). Then A = binom(n,3) - binom(n,3) = 0. Which is the minimum.\n\nTherefore, to minimize sum binom(d_j,2), we need a sequence where the d_j's are as small as possible.\n\nIf we arrange the sequence such that for each j, x_j is different from as many surrounding bits as possible. For example, a sequence with maximum alternation.\n\nBut for an odd n, the optimal sequence would be to have as many alternations as possible. For example, for n=5, the sequence 01010.\n\nLet\u2019s compute sum binom(d_j,2) for this sequence.\n\nSequence: 0,1,0,1,0\n\nCompute d_j for each position:\n\nj=1, x_j=0:\n\nd_1 = number of j<1 with x_j=0: 0\n\nnumber of j>1 with x_j\u22600: j>1: positions 2-5: x=1,0,1,0. So x_j\u22600: 1,1,0,0. Wait, x_j=0 at positions 3 and 5. So x_j\u22600 (i.e., 1) at positions 2 and 4. So number of j>1 with x_j\u22600 is 2.\n\nSo d_1=0+2=2.\n\nj=2, x_j=1:\n\nd_2 = number of j<2 with x_j=1: 0\n\nnumber of j>2 with x_j\u22601: positions 3-5: x=0,1,0. So x_j\u22601: 0 and 0. So 2.\n\nd_2=0+2=2.\n\nj=3, x_j=0:\n\nd_3 = number of j<3 with x_j=0: positions 1 and 3: but j<3, so posi