e entire figure is symmetric with respect to the origin. For instance, if you replace x with -x and y with -y in the equations, the equations transform into each other: y = kx + b becomes -y = -kx + b \u2192 y = kx - b, and similarly for the others. Therefore, the figure is symmetric about the origin, which implies that the origin is the center of the parallelogram, and hence the diagonals intersect at the origin.\n\nThis provides another perspective: the system is symmetric with respect to the origin, so the intersection point must be the origin.\n\nTherefore, after thorough analysis and multiple verification steps, the conclusion is that the diagonals intersect at (0, 0).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe coordinates of the point of intersection of the diagonals are \\boxed{(0, 0)}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the coordinates of the point of intersection of the diagonals of the quadrilateral formed by the intersection of the lines \\( y = kx + b \\), \\( y = kx - b \\), \\( y = mx + b \\), and \\( y = mx - b \\), we first determine the vertices of the quadrilateral by finding the intersection points of these lines.\n\n1. **Intersection of \\( y = kx + b \\) and \\( y = mx + b \\)**:\n   \\[\n   kx + b = mx + b \\implies x(k - m) = 0 \\implies x = 0 \\implies y = b\n   \\]\n   This gives the point \\((0, b)\\).\n\n2. **Intersection of \\( y = kx + b \\) and \\( y = mx - b \\)**:\n   \\[\n   kx + b = mx - b \\implies x(k - m) = -2b \\implies x = \\frac{-2b}{k - m}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( x \\) back into \\( y = mx - b \\):\n   \\[\n   y = m \\left( \\frac{-2b}{k - m} \\right) - b = \\frac{-2bm - b(k - m)}{k - m} = \\frac{-b(k + m)}{k - m}\n   \\]\n   This gives the point \\(\\left( \\frac{-2b}{k - m}, \\frac{-b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\).\n\n3. **Intersection of \\( y = kx - b \\) and \\( y = mx + b \\)**:\n   \\[\n   kx - b = mx + b \\implies x(k - m) = 2b \\implies x = \\frac{2b}{k - m}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( x \\) back into \\( y = mx + b \\):\n   \\[\n   y = m \\left( \\frac{2b}{k - m} \\right) + b = \\frac{2bm + b(k - m)}{k - m} = \\frac{b(k + m)}{k - m}\n   \\]\n   This gives the point \\(\\left( \\frac{2b}{k - m}, \\frac{b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\).\n\n4. **Intersection of \\( y = kx - b \\) and \\( y = mx - b \\)**:\n   \\[\n   kx - b = mx - b \\implies x(k - m) = 0 \\implies x = 0 \\implies y = -b\n   \\]\n   This gives the point \\((0, -b)\\).\n\nThe vertices of the quadrilateral are \\((0, b)\\), \\(\\left( \\frac{-2b}{k - m}, \\frac{-b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\), \\(\\left( \\frac{2b}{k - m}, \\frac{b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\), and \\((0, -b)\\).\n\nTo find the intersection of the diagonals, we calculate the midpoints of the diagonals. The diagonals are between \\((0, b)\\) and \\((0, -b)\\), and between \\(\\left( \\frac{-2b}{k - m}, \\frac{-b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\) and \\(\\left( \\frac{2b}{k - m}, \\frac{b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\).\n\n- **Midpoint of \\((0, b)\\) and \\((0, -b)\\)**:\n  \\[\n  \\left( \\frac{0 + 0}{2}, \\frac{b + (-b)}{2} \\right) = (0, 0)\n  \\]\n\n- **Midpoint of \\(\\left( \\frac{-2b}{k - m}, \\frac{-b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\) and \\(\\left( \\frac{2b}{k - m}, \\frac{b(k + m)}{k - m} \\right)\\)**:\n  \\[\n  \\left( \\frac{\\frac{-2b}{k - m} + \\frac{2b}{k - m}}{2}, \\frac{\\frac{-b(k + m)}{k - m} + \\frac{b(k + m)}{k - m}}{2} \\right) = (0, 0)\n  \\]\n\nBoth midpoints are \\((0, 0)\\), confirming that the diagonals intersect at the origin.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 0)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the type of figures formed by the given lines:**\n   - The given equations represent four linear functions:\n     \\[\n     y = kx + b, \\quad y = kx - b, \\quad y = mx + b, \\quad y = mx - b.\n     \\]\n     \n   - These lines form two pairs of parallel lines because:\n     - The lines \\(y = kx + b\\) and \\(y = kx - b\\) are parallel, both having the same slope \\(k\\).\n     - The lines \\(y = mx + b\\) and \\(y = mx - b\\) are parallel, both having the same slope 