ation as:\n\n[(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)] x\u00b2 - [2a h\u00b2] x - [a\u00b2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2)] = 0\n\nLet me denote coefficients:\n\nA = b\u00b2 - h\u00b2\n\nB = -2a h\u00b2\n\nC = -a\u00b2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nThen, the solutions are:\n\nx = [2a h\u00b2 \u00b1 sqrt{(2a h\u00b2)^2 - 4 (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)(-a\u00b2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2))}]/[2(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)]\n\nSimplify discriminant:\n\nD = (4a\u00b2 h^4) + 4 (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)(a\u00b2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2))\n\nFactor out 4a\u00b2:\n\nD = 4a\u00b2 [ h^4 + (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)(h\u00b2 + b\u00b2) ]\n\nCompute the term in brackets:\n\nh^4 + (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)(b\u00b2 + h\u00b2) = h^4 + (b^4 - h^4) = b^4\n\nTherefore, discriminant D = 4a\u00b2 b^4\n\nTherefore, x = [2a h\u00b2 \u00b1 2a b\u00b2]/[2(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)] = [a h\u00b2 \u00b1 a b\u00b2]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2) = a [h\u00b2 \u00b1 b\u00b2]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)\n\nSo, two solutions:\n\nx\u2081 = a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)\n\nx\u2082 = a (h\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2) = -a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2) = -a\n\nWait, x\u2082 = -a. But x = -a corresponds to point A. So line AC passes through A and C, and intersects hyperbola at point A and another point E. But the problem states the hyperbola intersects AC at E, so maybe E is the other intersection point. But in that case, E would have x-coordinate x\u2081 = a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)\n\nSimilarly for line BC: substituting into hyperbola, we can find F.\n\nLet me do the same for line BC: y = (-h/a)(x - a)\n\nSubstitute into hyperbola:\n\nx\u00b2/a\u00b2 - [ (h\u00b2/a\u00b2)(x - a)^2 ] / b\u00b2 = 1\n\nMultiply through by a\u00b2 b\u00b2:\n\nb\u00b2 x\u00b2 - h\u00b2 (x - a)^2 = a\u00b2 b\u00b2\n\nExpand (x - a)^2: x\u00b2 - 2a x + a\u00b2\n\nSo,\n\nb\u00b2 x\u00b2 - h\u00b2 x\u00b2 + 2a h\u00b2 x - h\u00b2 a\u00b2 = a\u00b2 b\u00b2\n\nGrouping terms:\n\n(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2) x\u00b2 + 2a h\u00b2 x - h\u00b2 a\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 = 0\n\nSame quadratic coefficients as line AC but with B coefficient positive 2a h\u00b2.\n\nThus, solutions:\n\nx = [ -2a h\u00b2 \u00b1 sqrt{(2a h\u00b2)^2 - 4 (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)( -a\u00b2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ))} ]/[2(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)]\n\nWait, the discriminant will be the same as before, 4a\u00b2 b^4.\n\nTherefore, solutions:\n\nx = [ -2a h\u00b2 \u00b1 2a b\u00b2 ]/[2(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)] = [ -a h\u00b2 \u00b1 a b\u00b2 ]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSo,\n\nx\u2081 = [ -a h\u00b2 + a b\u00b2 ]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2)/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = a\n\nx\u2082 = [ -a h\u00b2 - a b\u00b2 ]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nThus, the two intersection points on BC are x = a (which is point B) and x = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, the other intersection point F (excluding point B) is at x = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nWait, but that seems like the same as point E's x-coordinate but with a negative sign? Wait, no, let me check.\n\nFor line AC, the other intersection point E is at x = a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nFor line BC, the other intersection point F is at x = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nWait, that can't be right. Let me check:\n\nWait for line BC, the equation after substituting gives x = [ -a h\u00b2 \u00b1 a b\u00b2 ]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSo x\u2081 = [ -a h\u00b2 + a b\u00b2 ]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = a, which is point B.\n\nx\u2082 = [ -a h\u00b2 - a b\u00b2 ]/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, coordinates of F: x = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ), and y = (-h/a)(x - a )\n\nPlugging x into y:\n\ny = (-h/a)[ -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) - a ] = (-h/a)[ -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ]\n\nWait, let me compute x - a:\n\nx - a = [ -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ] - a = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = -a (2 b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, y = (-h/a)( -a (2 b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ) = (-h/a)( -2 a b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ) = (2 h b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSo coordinates of F:\n\nx_F = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\ny_F = 2 h b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly, for point E on line AC:\n\nx_E = a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\ny_E = (h/a)(x_E + a ) = (h/a)( a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) + a ) = (h/a)( a [ (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) + (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ] / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) )\n\nSimplify numerator inside: (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) + (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = 2 b\u00b2\n\nTherefore, y_E = (h/a)( a * 2 b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ) = (h/a)( 2 a b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ) = 2 h b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSo coordinates of E:\n\nx_E = a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\ny_E = 2 h b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nInterestingly, points E and F have the same y-coordinate, which is 2 h b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nMoreover, their x-coordinates are symmetric with respect to the y-axis: x_E is positive, x_F is negative, with the same magnitude.\n\nSo, points E and F are symmetric across the y-axis.\n\nNow, let's write the equations of the tangents at E and F.\n\nUsing the tangent line formula for hyperbola: (x x\u2080)/a\u00b2 - (y y\u2080)/b\u00b2 = 1\n\nFor point E (x_E, y_E):\n\nEquation of tangent: (x * x_E)/a\u00b2 - (y * y_E)/b\u00b2 = 1\n\nSimilarly for point F (x_F, y_F):\n\nEquation of tangent: (x * x_F)/a\u00b2 - (y * y_F)/b\u00b2 = 1\n\nLet me compute these equations.\n\nFirst, compute (x_E)/a\u00b2 and (y_E)/b\u00b2.\n\nx_E = a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nThus, x_E / a\u00b2 = (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) )\n\nSimilarly, y_E = 2 h b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nThus, y_E / b\u00b2 = 2 h / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, tangent at E:\n\nx * (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )) - y * 2 h / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) to eliminate denominators:\n\nx (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) / a - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly, for tangent at F:\n\nx_F = -a (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nThus, x_F / a\u00b2 = - (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) )\n\ny_F = 2 h b\u00b2 / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nThus, y_F / b\u00b2 = 2 h / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, tangent at F:\n\nx * [ - (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 )/(a (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )) ] - y * [ 2 h / (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) ] = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ):\n\n- x (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) / a - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nSo, the equations of the two tangents are:\n\nFor E: (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nFor F: - (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nNow, let's find the intersection point P of these two tangent lines.\n\nWe have two equations:\n\n1) (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\n2) - (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nLet me subtract equation 2 from equation 1:\n\n[ (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y ] - [ - (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y ] = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 ) - (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nLeft side:\n\n(h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a - 2 h y + (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a + 2 h y = [ 2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a ] = 0\n\nRight side: 0\n\nTherefore, 2 (h\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) x / a = 0 => x = 0\n\nSo, the x-coordinate of P is 0. That's the key point! Therefore, P lies on the y-axis, which is the line x=0. Since point C is at (0, h), the line PC is the vertical line x=0, hence PC is perpendicular to AB (which is along the x-axis). Therefore, PC is perpendicular to AB as required.\n\nNow, let's find the y-coordinate of P. Substitute x=0 into one of the tangent equations, say equation 1:\n\n0 - 2 h y = (b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 )\n\nThus, -2 h y = b\u00b2 - h\u00b2 => y = (h\u00b2 - b\u00b2 )/(2 h )\n\nTherefore, point P is at (0, (h\u00b2 - b\u00b2 )/(2 h )).\n\nBut point C is at (0, h). So PC connects (0, h) to (0, (h\u00b2 - b\u00b2 )/(2 h )). The line PC is indeed vertical, so it's perpendicular to AB.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.\n\nBut let me just verify if all steps are correct.\n\n1. Set coordinate system with AB on x-axis, C on y-axis. Correct.\n\n2. Found equations of lines AC and BC. Correct.\n\n3. Solved for intersections E and F with hyperbola. The algebra might be tedious, but discriminant simplified correctly, leading to x-coordinates for E and F. Then found coordinates for E and F. Noticed symmetry in coordinates, which is helpful.\n\n4. Derived equations for tangents at E and F using standard hyperbola tangent formula. Correct.\n\n5. Set up system of equations for tangents and solved for intersection point P. Found x=0, hence P is on y-axis. Therefore, PC is vertical, hence perpendicular to AB. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning seems solid. I don't see any errors in the steps. The key insight was realizing that the intersection point P of the tangents must lie on the y-axis, making PC vertical and hence perpendicular to AB.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the tangents at points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) on the hyperbola intersect at a point \\(P\\) such that \\(PC \\perp AB\\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup**:\n   - Place \\(A\\) at \\((-a, 0)\\), \\(B\\) at \\((a, 0)\\), and \\(C\\) at \\((0, h)\\).\n   - The hyperbola with real axis \\(AB\\) has the equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\).\n\n2. **Equations of Lines \\(AC\\) and \\(BC\\)**:\n   - Line \\(AC\\): \\(y = \\frac{h}{a}(x + a)\\).\n   - Line \\(BC\\): \\(y = -\\frac{h}{a}(x - a)\\).\n\n3. **Find Intersection Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)**:\n   - Substitute \\(y = \\frac{h}{a}(x + a)\\) into the hyperbola equation to solve for \\(E\\). After simplification, the coordinates of \\(E\\) are \\(\\left(\\frac{a(h^2 + b^2)}{b^2 - h^2}, \\frac{2hb^2}{b^2 - h^2}\\right)\\).\n   - Similarly, substituting \\(y = -\\frac{h}{a}(x - a)\\) gives coordinates of \\(F\\) as \\(\\left(-\\frac{a(h^2 + b^2)}{b^2 - h^2}, \\frac{2hb^2}{b^2 - h^2}\\right)\\).\n\n4. **Equations of Tangents at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)**:\n   - Tangent at \\(E\\): \\(\\frac{x \\cdot x_E}{a^2} - \\frac{y \\cdot y_E}{b^2} = 1\\). Substituting \\(E\\) gives \\(\\frac{(h^2 + b^2)x}{a(b^2 - h^2)} - \\frac{2hy}{b^2 - h^2} = 1\\).\n   - Tangent at \\(F\\): \\(\\frac{x \\cdot x_F}{a^2} - \\frac{y \\cdot y_F}{b^2} = 1\\). Substituting \\(F\\) gives \\(-\\frac{(h^2 + b^2)x}{a(b^2 - h^2)} - \\frac{2hy}{b^2 - h^2} = 1\\).\n\n5. **Intersection Point \\(P\\)**:\n   - Solving the system of tangent equations:\n     - Adding and subtracting the equations shows \\(x = 0\\).\n     - Substituting \\(x = 0\\) into either tangent equation gives \\(y = \\frac{h^2 - b^2}{2h}\\).\n   - Thus, \\(P = \\left(0, \\frac{h^2 - b^2}{2h}\\right)\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - Point \\(P\\) lies on the y-axis (\\(x = 0\\)), the same vertical line as \\(C(0, h)\\). Hence, \\(PC\\) is vertical and perpendicular to \\(AB\\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe tangents at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) intersect at point \\(P\\) on the y-axis, making \\(PC\\) vertical and thus perpendicular to \\(AB\\). Therefore, \\(PC \\perp AB\\), as required.  \n\\(\\boxed{PC \\perp AB}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Defining the Problem:**\n   - Given a hyperbola with the equation \\( b^{2} x^{2} - a^{2} y^{2} = a^{2} b^{2} \\).\n   - Points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) lie on the x-axis with coordinates given by \\( A = (-a,0) \\) and \\( B = (a, 0) \\).\n   \n2. **Coordinate of Points \\( E \\) and \\( F \\):**\n   - Let \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) be points on the hyperbola (or on its extensions) intersecting the lines \\( AC \\) and \\( BC \\), respectively. \n   - Assume \\( E \\) has coordinates \\( (a \\sec \\varphi, b \\tan \\varphi) \\), and \\( F \\) has coordinates \\( (a \\sec \\theta, b \\tan \\theta) \\).\n\n3. **Equations of the Lines Through \\( E \\) and \\( F \\):**\n   - The equation of the lines passing through \\( E \\) and \\( F \\), intersecting the rest of the sides of the triangle:\n     \\[\n     b^{2} a \\sec \\varphi x - a^{2} b \\tan \\varphi y = a^{2} b^{2},\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     b^{2} a \\sec \\theta x - a^{2} b \\tan \\theta y = a^{2} b^{2}.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Solving for the Intersection Point \\( P \\):**\n   - To find the x-coordinate \\( x_{P} \\) of point \\( P \\) where the lines intersect, we solve the system of equations:\n     \\[\n     x_{P} = \\frac{a (\\tan \\theta - \\tan \\varphi)}{\\sec \\varphi \\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta \\tan \\varphi}.\n     \\]\n\n5. **Equations of Lines \\( AE \\) and \\( BF \\):**\n   - The equations of the lines through points \\( A \\) and \\( E \\) and through \\( B \\) and \\( F \\) are derived respectively as:\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{b \\tan \\varphi}{a (\\sec \\varphi + 1)} (x + a),\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{b \\tan \\theta}{a (\\sec \\theta - 1)} (x - a).\n     \\]\n\n6. **Coordinate Calculation for the x-coordinate of Point \\( C \\):**\n   - Solving these line equations will help us determine the x-coordinate \\( x_{C} \\) of point \\( C \\):\n     \\[\n     x_{C} = -\\frac{a \\left[ (\\sec \\varphi + 1) \\tan \\theta + (\\sec \\theta - 1) \\tan \\varphi \\right]}{(\\sec \\theta - 1) \\tan \\varphi - (\\sec \\varphi + 1) \\tan \\theta}.\n     \\]\n\n7. **Simplifying to Show \\( x_{P} = x_{C} \\):**\n   - The numerator simplifies as follows:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Numerator} = a [\\sin (\\theta - \\varphi)(\\sin \\theta - \\sin \\varphi) + \\sin^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\varphi - \\sin (\\theta + \\varphi) \\sin (\\theta - \\varphi)] - \\sin (\\theta - \\varphi)(\\sin \\theta - \\sin \\varphi)\n     \\]\n     Simplifying further:\n     \\[\n     = a \\left(\\sin^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\varphi - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 2 \\varphi + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 2 \\theta \\right) = 0.\n     \\]\n\n     The denominator:\n     \\[\n     \\t