alysis, we establish that \\( BK = 2DC \\) by leveraging the given angle conditions and properties of isosceles triangles. \n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\boxed{BK = 2DC}", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Define relevant angles:**\n\n   Let $\\angle ABC = \\alpha$. From the given conditions, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAD = \\angle KAD = \\angle AKD = \\alpha\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Determine $\\angle BDC$ and $\\angle BAK$:**\n\n   Since $\\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ$ in $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\angle C = 90^\\circ$, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAB = 90^\\circ - \\alpha\n   \\]\n   \n   Thus, in $\\triangle ABD$, $\\angle BDC = 180^\\circ - \\angle ADB$:\n   \\[\n   \\angle BDC = 180^\\circ - (90^\\circ - \\alpha) = 90^\\circ + \\alpha\n   \\]\n   \n   Also, in $\\triangle AKD$, $\\angle BAK = 90^\\circ - 2\\alpha$.\n\n3. **Use the Law of Sines in $\\triangle ABK$:**\n\n   According to the Law of Sines:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BK}{\\sin \\angle BAK} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle AKB}\n   \\]\n   \n   Substitute $\\angle BAK$ and $\\angle AKB$:\n   \\[\n   BK = AB \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(90^\\circ - 2\\alpha)}{\\sin \\alpha}\n   \\]\n   \n   Since $\\sin(90^\\circ - 2\\alpha) = \\cos 2\\alpha$, we get:\n   \\[\n   BK = AB \\cdot \\frac{\\cos 2\\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use the Law of Sines in $\\triangle ABD$:**\n\n   According to the Law of Sines again:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BD}{\\sin \\angle BAD} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle ADB}\n   \\]\n   \n   Substitute $\\angle BAD$ and $\\angle ADB$:\n   \\[\n   BD = AB \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(90^\\circ - \\alpha)}{\\sin(180^\\circ - 2\\alpha)}\n   \\]\n   \n   Since $\\sin(90^\\circ - \\alpha) = \\cos \\alpha$ and $\\sin(180^\\circ - 2\\alpha) = \\sin 2\\alpha$, we get:\n   \\[\n   BD = AB \\cdot \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin 2\\alpha}\n   \\]\n   \n   Using the double angle identity $\\sin 2\\alpha = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha$, we have:\n   \\[\n   BD = AB \\cdot \\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha} = AB 