 \\angle OCD\\), we know that:\n\\[\n\\angle CDA = \\angle ONA \\quad \\text{and therefore,} \\quad \\angle CMO = \\angle ONA\n\\]\n\n5. Thus, triangles \\(OAN\\) and \\(OCM\\) are similar by AA similarity criterion (Angle-Angle similarity). From this similarity, we derive the proportion:\n\\[\n\\frac{AN}{CM} = \\frac{ON}{OM}\n\\]\n\n6. Similarly, in the triangles \\(OBL\\) and \\(ODN\\), the angles at \\(O\\) corresponding to the acute triangles are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle OLB = \\angle OND\n\\]\n\n7. Since triangles \\(OBL\\) and \\(ODM\\) are also similar (using the proportion found above):\n\\[\n\\frac{BL}{OL} = \\frac{ON}{OM} = \\frac{OD}{DM}\n\\]\n\n8. Consequently, the interior angles formed are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle LBO = \\angle ODM\n\\]\n\nFinally, this implies:\n\\[\n\\angle OBC = \\angle ODC\n\\]\n\nHence, we verify the result once again:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle OBC = \\angle ODC}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Is there a positive integer which can be written as the sum of 1990 consecutive positive integers and which can be written as a sum of two or more consecutive positive integers in exactly 1990 ways?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. The question is asking whether there exists a positive integer that can be written both as the sum of 1990 consecutive positive integers and can also be expressed as a sum of two or more consecutive positive integers in exactly 1990 different ways. Hmm, that sounds quite complex, but let's break it down.\n\nFirst, let me recall some number theory related to expressing numbers as sums of consecutive integers. I remember that a number can be expressed as a sum of consecutive integers in as many ways as it has odd divisors. Wait, no, more precisely, the number of ways a number can be expressed as a sum of consecutive positive integers is equal to the number of odd divisors of that number greater than 1