 a = 1 \\) and solving for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) led to specific solutions but was not sufficient for infinitely many solutions.\n\n3. **Recursive solutions**: By fixing two variables and solving for the third, we found that \\( m = 12 \\) allows for generating new solutions from existing ones. For example:\n   - Starting with \\( (a, b, c) = (2, 3, 7) \\), we generated solutions like \\( (2, 7, 5) \\), \\( (3, 7, 11) \\), and \\( (7, 11, 26) \\).\n   - This recursive process suggests an infinite chain of solutions by repeatedly solving for new values of \\( c \\).\n\n4. **Verification**: Checking these solutions confirmed that they satisfy the original equation with \\( m = 12 \\), indicating that new solutions can be generated indefinitely.\n\nThus, the key insight is that \\( m = 12 \\) allows for an infinite sequence of solutions through recursive generation, leading to the conclusion that such an \\( m \\) exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{abc} = \\frac{m}{a + b + c}\n\\]\nand asked whether there exists an integer \\( m \\) for which this equation has infinitely many solutions in positive integers \\( a, b, \\) and \\( c \\).\n\nFirst, let\u2019s test the simple case where \\( a = b = c = 1 \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1} = 4\n\\]\n\\[\na + b + c = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3\n\\]\nSo, we need \\( \\frac{m}{3} = 4 \\implies m = 12 \\).\n\nTherefore, we set \\( m = 12 \\), then our equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{abc} = \\frac{12}{a + b + c}\n\\]\n\nRewriting the left side of the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{Abc + Bac + Cab + 1}{abc} = \\frac{12}{a + b + c}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by \\( abc \\) then \\( a + b + c \\):\n\\[\na + b + c + \\frac{1}{abc} = \\frac{12abc}{a + b + c}\n\\]\n\nInstead, let\u2019s consider polynomial \\( p(a, b, c) \\):\n\\[\np(a, b, c) = a^2(b + c) + b^2(c + a) + c^2(a + b) + a + b + c - 9abc\n\\]\nSubstituting back and simplifying:\n\\[\n(b + c) a^2 + \\left(b^2 + c^2 - 9bc + 1\\right) a + (b + c)(bc + 1) = 0\n\\]\n\\( p(x, b, c) = 0 \\) has \\( x \\leq b \\leq c \\).\n\nThis quadratic equation in terms of \\( x \\) implies by Vieta\u2019s formulas:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{bc + 1}{x}\n\\]\n\nDefine the sequence \\( \\{a_n\\} \\) where:\n\\[\na_0 = a_1 = a_2 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_{n+2} = \\frac{a_{n}a_{n+1}+1}{a_{n-1}} \\text{ for } n \\geq 1\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\( \\{a_n\\} (n=2,3,\\ldots) \\) is an increasing sequence of positive integers where \\( p(a_{n-1}, a_n, a_{n+1}) = 0 \\).\n\nIn fact, \n\\[\na_{n+2}a_{n-1} - a_na_{n+1} = 1 \\text{ (constant)}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\na_{n+2}a_{n-1} - a_na_{n+1} = a_{n+1} a_{n-2} - a_{n-1} a_n\n\\]\n\nGiven all \\(a_n > 0 \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a_{n+2} + a_n}{a_{n+1}} = \\frac{a_n + a_{n-2}}{a_{n-1}}\n\\]\n\nThus, \nif \\( n \\) is even:\n\\[\n\\frac{a_n + a_{n-2}}{a_{n-1}} = \\frac{a_2 + a_0}{a_1} = 2\n\\]\nif \\( n \\) is odd:\n\\[\n\\frac{a_n + a_{n-2}}{a_{n-1}} = \\frac{a_3 + a_1}{a_2} = 3\n\\]\n\nHence, the recursive formulas:\n\\[\na_n = \n\\begin{cases} \n2a_{n-1} - a_{n-2} & \\text{if n is even} \\\\\n3a_{n-1} - a_{n-2} & \\text{if n is odd} \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nStarting from \\( a_0 = a_1 = a_2 = 1 \\), using mathematical induction, verifies \\( \\{a_n\\} \\).\n\nTherefore, for \\( m = 12 \\), the equation has infinitely many integer solutions where \\( (a, b, c) = (a_{n-1}, a_n, a_{n+1}) \\) for \\( n = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots \\).\n\nSo, the conclusion is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The lengths of the sides of a triangle form an arithmetic progression.\n\nProve that the radius of the inscribed circle is equal to one-third of one of the heights of the triangle.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that