 one-third of the height corresponding to the middle side, which is a.\n\nHence, the proof is complete.\n\nBut let me check if there's another way to approach this problem, maybe by considering specific values or another formula.\n\nAlternatively, let's recall that in any triangle, the area can also be expressed as r*s, where r is the inradius and s is the semiperimeter.\n\nWe also know that the area is (base * height)/2. So, if we take the middle side a as the base, then the area is (a * h2)/2. Therefore, equating the two expressions for area:\n\nr*s = (a * h2)/2\n\nBut s = 3a/2, so:\n\nr*(3a/2) = (a * h2)/2 \u21d2 3a/2 * r = a h2 /2 \u21d2 3r = h2 \u21d2 r = h2 /3\n\nHence, this directly shows that r is one-third of the height corresponding to the middle side a.\n\nWow, that's a much simpler approach! So instead of going through Heron's formula, we can use the relation A = r*s and A = (base * height)/2 for any side as the base.\n\nTherefore, choosing the middle side as the base, we have A = (a * h2)/2 and A = r*(3a/2). Equating these gives r = h2 /3.\n\nThis is a more straightforward proof without getting into the complexities of Heron's formula. So, regardless of the sides being in arithmetic progression, this relation holds for the middle side. But wait, does this hold for any triangle?\n\nWait, in any triangle, the inradius times the semiperimeter equals the area, which also equals (base * height)/2. So, for any side, if you take that side as the base, then r*s = (base * height)/2. Therefore, r = (base * height)/(2s). So, in general, for each height, r = (base * height)/(2s). Therefore, unless the base and semiperimeter have a specific relation, the inradius isn't necessarily a fraction of the height. But in this case, because the sides are in arithmetic progression, choosing the middle side as the base gives us base = a, and semiperimeter s = 3a/2. Therefore:\n\nr = (a * h2)/(2*(3a/2)) = (a * h2)/3a = h2/3.\n\nThus, immediately, r = h2/3.\n\nSo, this seems to 