al.\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about this is that the set of rational points on a circle (with at least one rational point) is dense in the circle, so there are infinitely many. But the problem likely expects the parametric solution as demonstration.\n\nLet me test this with a specific example to ensure it works. Let's choose a rational slope, say m = 0. Then the line through A(42,12) with slope 0 is horizontal: y = 12. Let's find the other intersection point with the circle.\n\nSubstitute y = 12 into the circle equation:\n\n\\( (x +1)^2 + 12^2 = 1993 \\)\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( (x +1)^2 + 144 = 1993 \\)\n\\( (x +1)^2 = 1993 -144 = 1849 \\)\n\\( x +1 = \\pm 43 \\)\nThus, x = -1 +43 = 42 or x = -1 -43 = -44\n\nSo the other intersection point is (-44, 12). Let's check if this is on the circle:\n\nLeft-hand side: (-44)^2 + 2*(-44) + 12^2 = 1936 -88 + 144 = 1936 -88 = 1848; 1848 +144 = 1992. Wait, but the original equation is \\( x^2 +2x + y^2 =1992 \\). So yes, 1992. But our transformed equation was \\( (x +1)^2 + y^2 = 1993 \\). Let me verify:\n\nFor x = -44, y =12:\n\n\\( (-44 +1)^2 + 12^2 = (-43)^2 + 144 = 1849 +144 = 1993 \\). Correct. But in the original equation, x^2 +2x + y^2 = (-44)^2 +2*(-44) +12^2 = 1936 -88 +144 = 1936 -88 = 1848 +144 = 1992. So yes, it satisfies the original equation. Thus, the point (-44,12) is on the circle and has integer coordinates, hence rational.\n\nSo using m =0 gives another rational point. Let's use another slope, say m =1.\n\nThen the line is y -12 =1*(x -42) => y = x -30.\n\nSubstitute into the circle equation:\n\n\\( (x +1)^2 + (x -30)^2 =1993 \\)\n\nExpand:\n\n\\( x\u00b2 +2x +1 +x\u00b2 -60x +900 =1993 \\)\n\nCombine terms:\n\n2x\u00b2 -58x +901 =1993\n\n2x\u00b2 -58x +901 -1993=0 => 2x\u00b2 -58x -1092=0\n\nDivide by 2:\n\nx\u00b2 -29x -546=0\n\nSolve using quadratic formula:\n\nx = [29 \u00b1 sqrt(841 +2184)] /2 = [29 \u00b1 sqrt(3025)] /2 = [29 \u00b155]/2\n\nThus, x = (29 +55)/2 =84/2=42 (which is point A) or x=(29